<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="no"?>
<!--
  Target configuration for the CC2652P + CP2102N USB dongle, driven through the
  LAUNCHXL-CC26X2R1's XDS110 as an EXTERNAL target.

  NO TWO CONSECUTIVE HYPHENS ANYWHERE IN THIS COMMENT. That is an XML rule,
  not a style choice, and it is why cc2538-bsl's option names below are spelled
  out in prose instead of copied. Break it and dslite says

      Failed: Cannot read System Setup data from XML file <path>

  which reads as a missing or unreadable file and sends you looking at paths
  and permissions rather than at the comment you just wrote. Measured here,
  2026-08-19, on the first run of this file.

  WHY AN EXTERNAL PROBE AT ALL. This dongle's own CC2652P cannot be reached any
  other way on this bench, and that is measured rather than assumed:

    * The ROM serial bootloader does not answer. cc2538-bsl was run with all
      four of its auto-BSL wirings (its default, plus the invert-lines,
      active-high and sonoff-usb options), and scripts/ti/bsl_probe.py
      swept both modem lines at both polarities, plus 75 s of sync-spam while a
      human worked all three buttons. Every one: "Timeout waiting for ACK/NACK
      after 'Synch (0x55 0x55)'". E72-class CC2652P modules ship with the CCFG
      backdoor disabled - their own documentation says the first flash must go
      over JTAG - so this is the module behaving as designed, not a fault.
    * The nRF5340 DK's J-Link OB cannot substitute. It sees the target
      (VTref=3.300V) and then refuses the only protocol the part speaks:
      "cJTAG is not supported by connected J-Link". CC26x2 has no SWD DP at
      all; see scripts/flash_ti.ps1's header for the four dead ends measured on
      the LaunchXL.

  So the XDS110 on the LaunchXL is the one probe on this bench that can do it,
  driving the dongle through the isolation block with all five shunts removed.

  WHAT DIFFERS FROM cc26x2r1_launchxl.ccxml - exactly one thing, the device:
  CC2652P1F rather than CC2652R1F. The P part is the same die family with an
  integrated PA. Everything else in that file's header applies unchanged and is
  not repeated here; in particular "SWD Mode Settings" = 4 (cJTAG 2-pin) is
  still stated explicitly on the CONNECTION, because DebugServer takes it from
  there and connections/TIXDS110_Connection.xml defaults it to plain 4-pin
  JTAG. Omitting it produces "IcePick_C: Error connecting to the target:
  (Error -242 @ 0x0)", which reads like a wrong device.

  POWER SELECTION IS DELIBERATELY LEFT ALONE. The dongle powers itself from its
  own USB port, so the XDS110 must NOT supply 3V3 - two sources fighting on one
  rail. The device file's default is "Target supplied power"; do not add a
  Power Selection property here to override it.
-->
<configurations XML_version="1.2" id="configurations_0">
    <configuration XML_version="1.2" id="configuration_0">
        <instance XML_version="1.2" desc="Texas Instruments XDS110 USB Debug Probe" href="connections/TIXDS110_Connection.xml" id="Texas Instruments XDS110 USB Debug Probe" xml="TIXDS110_Connection.xml" xmlpath="connections"/>
        <connection XML_version="1.2" desc="Texas Instruments XDS110 USB Debug Probe" id="Texas Instruments XDS110 USB Debug Probe">
            <!--
              The three driver bindings for this device's debug topology, in
              the order the scan chain presents them: the ICEPick-C router, the
              CoreSight DAP behind it, and the Cortex-M core behind that.
              Without these three lines DebugServer reports "An invalid
              processor ID has been found", which reads like a wrong device
              rather than a missing binding.
            -->
            <instance XML_version="1.2" href="drivers/tixds510icepick_c.xml" id="drivers" xml="tixds510icepick_c.xml" xmlpath="drivers"/>
            <instance XML_version="1.2" href="drivers/tixds510cs_dap.xml" id="drivers" xml="tixds510cs_dap.xml" xmlpath="drivers"/>
            <instance XML_version="1.2" href="drivers/tixds510cortexM.xml" id="drivers" xml="tixds510cortexM.xml" xmlpath="drivers"/>
            <property Type="choicelist" Value="4" id="SWD Mode Settings">
                <choice Name="cJTAG (1149.7) 2-pin advanced modes" value="enable">
                    <property Type="choicelist" Value="1" id="XDS110 Aux Port"/>
                </choice>
            </property>
            <platform XML_version="1.2" id="platform_0">
                <instance XML_version="1.2" desc="CC2652P1F" href="devices/cc2652p1f.xml" id="CC2652P1F" xml="cc2652p1f.xml" xmlpath="devices"/>
                <device HW_revision="1" XML_version="1.2" desc="CC2652P1F" description="SimpleLink(TM) multi-protocol CC2652P wireless MCU with integrated PA" id="CC2652P1F" partnum="CC2652P1F" simulation="no"/>
            </platform>
        </connection>
    </configuration>
</configurations>
